// Source text for the in-app guide at /guide. Plain Markdown in a string so it
// ships with the build (no file reads at runtime). Edit the text here.
export const userGuideMarkdown: string = "# UK Personal Tax Calculator — user guide\n\n## What this app does\n\nThe UK Personal Tax Calculator estimates your income tax, National\nInsurance and Capital Gains Tax for the tax year you choose, bringing\nyour salary, pension, self-employment, rental income, savings,\ndividends and gains together in one calculation. It also handles the\ndetails that are easy to get wrong:\n\n- itemised landlord and business expenses\n- mortgage interest relief\n- letting a furnished room in your own home (Rent-a-Room Relief)\n- Class 4 National Insurance for the self-employed\n- tax already withheld abroad on foreign dividends\n- capital losses, and the sale of your home\n- pension contributions and Gift Aid\n- the High Income Child Benefit Charge\n- student loan repayments\n- Marriage Allowance\n- Blind Person's Allowance and Married Couple's Allowance\n- Scotland's separate income tax bands (and Welsh tax codes)\n- a tax code estimate you can compare with the one HMRC gave you\n\nOn the welcome page, a **\"Hear how it works\"** button plays a short audio\noverview (about 80 seconds), with a written transcript underneath if you\nprefer to read it.\n\nYou'll need to sign in with an approved Google account to use it —\nyour organisation's administrator controls who's approved. Once signed\nin, you can save calculations, edit or delete them later, and pick up\nwhere you left off next time you visit.\n\n**It gives an estimate, not a tax return.** At the bottom of the page\nthere's a panel called *\"What this calculator doesn't cover\"* that\nlists everything deliberately left out, with advice on where to get\nproper help for each. See \"What this calculator doesn't cover\" below.\n\nThe app's **Terms of Service** and **Privacy Policy** are real,\nstandalone pages — linked from the footer, and readable without\nsigning in, since you should be able to check them before you pay or\nhand over an email address, not after.\n\n---\n\n## Getting started\n\n1. Open the app in your browser.\n2. **Sign in with Google.** Anyone can sign in — that part's always\n   open. Whether you can actually *use* the calculator afterwards\n   depends on whether your account is approved (see step 3).\n3. If your account isn't approved yet, you'll see a message saying so.\n   Depending on how this deployment is set up, you'll see either a\n   **Buy access** button, instructions to ask an administrator to add\n   your email, or both — see \"Buying access\" below if you see the\n   button.\n4. Once approved, enter your figures and click **Calculate**.\n5. Saved calculations, editing and deleting are all available once\n   you're signed in — see \"Managing your saved calculations\" below.\n6. **Everything is in pounds sterling**, and results are shown in whole\n   pounds (the calculation itself works to the penny).\n\n## Buying access\n\nIf this deployment accepts payment, you'll see a **Buy access** button\nwith a price on the \"not approved yet\" screen after you sign in.\n\n1. Click it — you're taken to a payment page hosted entirely by\n   Stripe, a payment processor. Your card details go to Stripe\n   directly; they never pass through or get stored by this app.\n2. Pay. You're returned to the app with a \"payment received\" message.\n3. It can take a few seconds for the payment to be recorded. If the\n   calculator doesn't appear straight away, wait a moment and refresh.\n4. That's it — no separate account, subscription, or renewal. It's a\n   one-time payment for ongoing access.\n\nIf you paid and it's still not unlocking after a minute or two, that\npoints to a configuration issue on the administrator's end rather than\na problem with your payment — Stripe will have taken payment\nregardless (check your email for their receipt) and can confirm this\nindependently of the app itself. Contact whoever administers the app.\n\n---\n\n## How the form is laid out\n\nThe form is split into coloured **cards**, one for each kind of income\nor adjustment, so you can see where one category ends and the next\nbegins. From top to bottom:\n\n1. **Tax year and region** (blue) — choose these first, because they\n   decide which rates are used\n2. **Employment** (amber) — your salary\n3. **Student loan**, **Marriage Allowance**, **Blind Person's Allowance**\n   and **Married Couple's Allowance** — always on the page,\n   because they apply to people with nothing but a salary\n4. A button reading **\"+ Add pension, self-employment, rental,\n   rent-a-room, savings, dividends, gains, reliefs or Child Benefit\"** —\n   click it to open the rest, in this order: Pension, Rental,\n   Rent-a-Room, Self-employment, Savings interest, Dividends, Capital\n   gains, Reliefs, Child Benefit\n5. **Calculate**, with **Clear all** beside it\n\n**Opening and closing cards.** Click a card's heading to open or close\nit. A card you've filled in stays open and shows a small summary next\nto its heading (the amount you entered, for instance), so you can see\nwhat's in each category at a glance even if you collapse it. Colours\ngroup related cards: income, savings and investments, and adjustments\nsuch as reliefs.\n\nLeave anything that doesn't apply to you blank. On a phone the cards\nuse the full width of the screen and the buttons are sized for easy\ntapping.\n\n**Starting over:** click **Clear all** to empty everything you've\nentered — every field, the ticked boxes, your student loan plan, and\nthe results — and close the extra sections. It asks you to confirm\nfirst if there's anything in the form, and it keeps your tax year and\nregion as they are. If you were editing a saved calculation, clearing\njust stops the editing; the saved calculation itself is untouched.\n\n---\n\n## The basics\n\n### Employment income (salary)\n\nEnter your **taxable pay** — the \"pay\" figure on your P60. That figure\nis already after any pension contributions taken from your payslip\nbefore tax, so don't add those back. It isn't your take-home pay\neither, which is lower again after tax and National Insurance.\n\n### Tax year and region\n\n- **Tax year**: the year your income relates to (e.g. `2026-27`).\n- **Region**: **England & NI**, **Wales** or **Scotland**. Only\n  Scotland changes the tax: it has its own bands for your salary,\n  pension, rental, and self-employment income. Wales has the same rates\n  as England, so choosing it doesn't change your tax figures. It only\n  gives your tax code the Welsh **C** prefix (see \"Checking your tax\n  code\" below). Savings, dividends, and capital gains are taxed the same\n  way UK-wide whichever you pick, because they aren't devolved. A\n  saved calculation remembers Scotland but not Wales, so one loaded from\n  your history opens as England & NI.\n\n### Student loan\n\nChoose your plan from the dropdown — **Plan 1, Plan 2, Plan 4\n(Scotland), or Plan 5** — or leave it on \"No undergraduate loan\". Not\nsure which you have? Your student loan statement, or your online\nstudent loan account on GOV.UK, will say.\n\nIf you also have a **Postgraduate Loan**, tick the box beneath. It can\nrun alongside an undergraduate plan, and both are repaid at once.\n\nHow it's worked out: **9%** of your income above your plan's threshold,\nplus **6%** of your income above £21,000 if you have a Postgraduate\nLoan. The 2026/27 thresholds:\n\n| Plan | Repayments start above |\n|---|---|\n| Plan 1 | £26,900 |\n| Plan 2 | £29,385 |\n| Plan 4 (Scotland) | £33,795 |\n| Plan 5 | £25,000 |\n| Postgraduate Loan | £21,000 |\n\n(These change most years. The app uses whatever figures your\nadministrator has entered for the year you pick.)\n\nTwo things worth knowing. The figure shown is the **whole year's**\nrepayment. If you're employed, your employer normally deducts it\nthrough payroll during the year, so it isn't an extra bill on top of\nwhat's already come out of your pay. And it's calculated on your total\nincome after pension contributions and Gift Aid, which is a sensible\nestimate rather than an exact copy of how HMRC treats each source.\n\n### Marriage Allowance\n\nTick this only if your spouse or civil partner is **transferring** their\nMarriage Allowance **to you**. It adds £1,260 (the 2026/27 amount) to\nyour Personal Allowance — worth up to £252 in tax to a basic-rate\ntaxpayer.\n\nTwo conditions apply that the app **can't check for you**:\n\n- Your partner must genuinely have unused Personal Allowance to give —\n  broadly, their own income is below £12,570 — and must apply to\n  transfer it.\n- **You** must be a basic-rate taxpayer. HMRC measures this on your\n  income *before* the transfer: £50,270 or less in the rest of the UK,\n  or £43,662 or less in Scotland. The app checks this side, and if your\n  income is above the limit the results show an amber warning that you\n  may not actually qualify.\n\n**If you're the partner giving the allowance away,** tick **\"I am giving\npart of my Marriage Allowance to my spouse or civil partner\"** instead.\nYou can tick one box or the other, not both. This takes £1,260 off your\nown Personal Allowance, so your partner can reduce their tax by up to\n£252.\n\nGOV.UK's condition for the person giving is that your income is **below\nyour own Personal Allowance** (£12,570). If it's at or above that\n(including exactly £12,570), the results show an amber warning that you\nmay not qualify.\n\nGiving the allowance away can cost you a little tax. Once you've given\n£1,260, you keep £11,310. Any income above that is taxed, and the\nresults tell you how much income that affects. Your partner's saving,\nup to £252, shows on their own calculation with the receiving box\nticked. The app can't add the two together for you, and it can't check\nthat your partner qualifies, so run their figures separately.\n\n### Blind Person's Allowance\n\nAn extra tax-free amount on top of your Personal Allowance, for people\nwho are registered as blind or severely sight impaired (England and\nWales), or whose sight stops them doing any work that needs eyesight\n(Scotland and Northern Ireland). Tick **I qualify for Blind Person's\nAllowance** to add it.\n\n- It doesn't depend on your income, and unlike the Personal Allowance it\n  **isn't reduced above £100,000**.\n- You have to claim it from HMRC. The app can't check that you qualify.\n- If your partner can't use all of their own allowance, they can pass the\n  unused part to you. Enter that amount in the optional box (it can't\n  exceed one full allowance).\n- If *you* have more allowance than your income needs, your results show\n  how much is unused and can be transferred to your spouse or civil\n  partner using HMRC form 575(T).\n- It is included in your estimated tax code.\n\n### Married Couple's Allowance\n\nOnly available if you are married or in a civil partnership, you live\nwith your partner, and at least one of you was born before 6 April\n1935. Tick **I am claiming Married Couple's Allowance** to apply it.\n\n- It isn't an allowance against income. It **reduces your tax bill** by\n  10% of a set amount, shown in your results.\n- The amount starts at a maximum and falls by £1 for every £2 of your\n  income above a limit, down to a minimum. The figures for each tax year\n  are set up by the administrator.\n- If you married before 5 December 2005 it normally goes to the husband.\n  Otherwise it normally goes to the partner with the higher income. Tick\n  the box only if you are the one claiming.\n- If your tax bill is smaller than the reduction, the unused part is shown\n  and can be transferred to your partner by asking HMRC.\n- Your estimated tax code doesn't include it, so your actual code may be\n  higher. The panel says so.\n- The app can't check that your marriage, ages or living arrangements\n  qualify.\n\n---\n\n## Other income and reliefs\n\nEverything below sits behind the **\"+ Add pension, self-employment, …\"**\nbutton.\n\n### Pension\n\nEnter State Pension, workplace pension, or private pension income in\n**Pension income**.\n\n**Why it's a separate field from salary:** pension income is taxed\nthrough exactly the same bands as salary, so it might seem to belong in\nEmployment income. It doesn't — Employment income is also used to work\nout National Insurance, and pensions never attract NI. Keeping it\nseparate means your income tax is right *and* you're correctly shown\n£0 National Insurance on it.\n\n### Rental\n\nEnter your total rental income in **Rental profit (before\nexpenses/allowance)**.\n\n**Allowance or itemised expenses — whichever pays off.** Rental income\ngets the flat **£1,000 property allowance** by default. Landlords can\noften do better by itemising their actual allowable expenses, and HMRC\nlets you use whichever is worth more. The app compares them for you.\nClick **\"+ Itemise allowable expenses\"** to enter amounts against:\n\n- Letting agent / management fees\n- Landlord insurance\n- Repairs & maintenance\n- Ground rent & service charges\n- Council tax & utility bills (during voids)\n- Legal & professional fees\n- Advertising & direct costs\n- Other allowable expenses\n\nYou don't have to choose. Enter your real expenses (even if they come\nto less than £1,000) and the results show which method was used.\n\n**Mortgage interest goes in its own field, not as an expense.** Since\n2020/21 it's no longer deductible from rental profit. Landlords instead\nget a flat **20% tax credit** on their final bill, whatever their tax\nband. Enter it in **Mortgage interest paid**. The credit is capped at\nthe lowest of your interest paid, your rental profit, and your income\nabove the Personal Allowance — so someone whose only income is a small\nrental profit may see little or no credit in that year (the unused\ninterest carries forward; see below).\n\n### Rent-a-Room\n\nUse this section if you let a **furnished room in your own home** to a\nlodger. It's a different relief from **Rental** above, and the two\ndon't mix: Rental is for a property you let out as a landlord, whereas\nthis is for the home you actually live in — whether you own it or rent\nit (if you rent, check that your own tenancy allows a lodger). If you\nhave both kinds of income, fill in both sections separately.\n\nEnter everything the lodger pays you in **Rent-a-Room receipts** — the\nrent plus any share of bills, meals, cleaning or laundry rolled into the\npayment. HMRC counts all of it towards the limit, not just the headline\nrent.\n\nWhat happens next depends on how much that comes to:\n\n- **Up to £7,500 a year** (the figure for both 2025/26 and 2026/27) is\n  tax-free automatically. There's nothing to claim, and you don't need\n  to tell HMRC or file a Self Assessment return for this income alone.\n  The results confirm it.\n- **Sharing the income** with someone else, such as a partner or joint\n  owner, halves the limit to **£3,750 each**. Tick the box under the\n  field and enter only *your own share* of the receipts — the app\n  applies the lower limit to whatever you type.\n- **Over the limit**, you must report the income on a Self Assessment\n  return, and there are two ways to work out what's taxable: the\n  limit method (tax on receipts above the limit, with no expenses\n  deducted), or tax on receipts minus your actual expenses. HMRC uses\n  actual expenses unless you tell it you want the limit method. The app\n  works out both and uses whichever leaves less to tax.\n\n**If actual expenses win.** Click **\"+ Itemise actual expenses\"** and\nfill in whatever applies: your share of utility bills, cleaning or\nlaundry, wear and tear on furnishings, advertising for a lodger, and\nanything else allowable. Expenses only make a difference once your\nreceipts are over the limit, and they have to be higher than the limit\nitself to beat it. If they do win, the results say so. That is the\nmethod HMRC uses by default, so there is nothing to tell them. If the\nlimit method wins instead, you have to ask HMRC for it yourself, within\none year of the 31 January Self Assessment deadline for that tax year.\nThe app can't do that for you.\n\n**Not modelled.** A *loss* from lodger income: if your expenses beat\nyour receipts, the taxable amount simply shows as £0 and nothing carries\nforward. You cannot create a loss under the limit method or the automatic\nexemption. To claim one you tell HMRC you want to be taxed on receipts\nminus expenses, so it's worth taking advice. Mortgage interest\nrelief on this income isn't modelled either.\n\n### Self-employment\n\nEnter your total business income (turnover) in **Self-employment income\n(before expenses/allowance)**.\n\nIt works like rental: the app applies either the flat **£1,000 trading\nallowance** or your itemised business expenses, whichever is worth\nmore. Click **\"+ Itemise business expenses\"** for these categories:\n\n- Cost of goods / materials\n- Office costs (stationery, phone, software)\n- Travel costs\n- Business premises: rent, rates, utilities\n- Staff costs\n- Advertising & marketing\n- Professional/legal/accountancy fees\n- Other allowable business expenses\n\nSelf-employment profit is taxed through the same income tax bands as\nsalary, but National Insurance differs: **Class 4** (6% on profit\nbetween £12,570 and £50,270, then 2% above), rather than the 8%/2%\nClass 1 rate on salary.\n\n**Class 2 National Insurance** costs most people nothing now — since\n2024/25 it's £0 once your profit clears the \"small profits threshold\"\nand simply protects your State Pension record. The app shows this as an\ninformational note. If your profit is below the threshold, the note\ntells you the small voluntary amount you could pay to protect your\npension record.\n\n### Vehicle costs (self-employed)\n\nInside the **Self-employment** card, **+ Vehicle costs** lets you claim\nthe cost of a car, van or motorcycle you use for work. Choose **one\nmethod per vehicle**, because you can't claim both on the same one. The\namount is added to your business expenses automatically, so don't also\nenter it in the itemised list.\n\n**Option 1: flat mileage rates (\"simplified expenses\").** Enter your\nbusiness miles. Cars and vans have one rate for the first 10,000 miles\nand a lower rate after that, and motorcycles have a single rate. The\nrates for each tax year are applied for you. For 2026-27 they are 55p\nand 25p for cars and vans, and 24p for motorcycles. Before 6 April 2026\nthe first 10,000 miles were 45p.\n\n**Option 2: capital allowances for one vehicle.** This claims the cost of\nthe vehicle itself.\n\n- **Bought this year:** enter the cost, whether it was new or\n  second-hand, and for a car its CO2 emissions (on the V5C registration\n  document).\n  - A **new zero-emission car** gets a 100% first-year allowance.\n  - Other cars are written off gradually. Cars up to 50 g/km go in the\n    **main pool** (14% a year from 6 April 2026, 18% before), and cars over\n    50 g/km go in the **special rate pool** (6% a year).\n  - **Vans and motorcycles** qualify for the Annual Investment Allowance, which\n    gives 100% relief in the year you buy them. That limit is shared with any\n    other equipment you buy, which the app doesn't track.\n- **Bought in an earlier year:** enter the value left to claim at the start of\n  this year (this is the \"value left to claim next year\" figure from last\n  year's results) and which pool it is in.\n- **Business use:** enter the share of use that is for business. Only that\n  share is allowed.\n- **Sold this year:** enter the sale proceeds. Selling for more than the\n  remaining value creates a taxable **balancing charge**, which is added to\n  your business income. For a vehicle with private use, selling for less\n  gives a **balancing allowance**.\n- **Small pools:** if the value left is £1,000 or less, the whole amount can\n  be claimed.\n\nYour results show the allowance, any balancing charge, and the **value to\ncarry into next year**. Keep that figure, as you would a loss.\n\nThis covers **one vehicle in one tax year**. Several vehicles, leased or\nhire-purchase vehicles and part-year accounts aren't covered, so see an\naccountant. Zero-emission 100% first-year relief is currently due to\nend on 5 April 2027, and rates can change, so check the figures for the\nyear you're in.\n\n### Savings interest\n\nEnter the total interest from your savings accounts for the tax year.\n**Leave out interest earned inside an ISA** — it's tax-free. The results\nshow how much was covered by your starting rate for savings and your\nPersonal Savings Allowance, both of which the app works out for you.\n\n### Dividends\n\nEnter total dividends in **Dividend income**. As with savings, leave out\nanything received inside an ISA or pension wrapper.\n\n**Tax already withheld abroad.** If you hold foreign shares — US blue\nchips are the classic example — that country may take tax off your\ndividends before you receive them. For US shares, a **W-8BEN** form\nfiled with your broker reduces the withholding from 30% to 15% under the\nUK-US treaty.\n\nYou still owe UK tax on the same dividend, but not twice. Enter what was\nwithheld in **Foreign tax already withheld on these dividends** and the\napp credits it against your UK dividend tax, following HMRC's rule\n(Helpsheet HS263): the credit is the *smaller* of what was withheld and\nthe UK tax actually due on that dividend. It can wipe out your UK tax on\nthat dividend, but never creates a refund, and any excess withheld\nabroad isn't recoverable through this relief.\n\n### Capital gains\n\nEverything here is in pounds. For shares priced in another currency,\nwork the gain out in sterling, using the exchange rate on the day you\nbought and the day you sold — a currency move can create a gain or a\nloss in sterling even if the share price didn't change.\n\nThe app applies the **£3,000 annual exempt amount** for you, and taxes\nwhat's left at **18%** or **24%**, depending on how much of your basic\nrate band is unused after your other income.\n\n**Selling your home** — use **Gain on selling your only or main home**.\nIf it was your only or main home for the *entire* time you owned it —\nno part let out, no business use, no periods when it wasn't your home —\ntick the box beneath. The gain is then fully exempt under **Private\nResidence Relief**, and it doesn't even use up your annual exempt\namount.\n\nIf any part of that isn't true, **leave the box unticked**. The app\ncan't calculate partial relief, so it taxes the whole gain as if none\nof it were exempt. That will usually *overstate* your bill — treat it as\na cautious estimate, and take advice on a sale with a let period or\nbusiness use.\n\n**Other capital gains** is the total gain (sale price minus what you\npaid, plus allowable costs) across everything else you sold.\n\n**Capital losses this year** — anything you sold at a loss in the same\ntax year. These *must* be set against this year's gains in full; you\ncan't choose to save them for later.\n\n**Capital losses brought forward from earlier years** — losses from\nprevious years. These are used only if a taxable gain is left *after*\nthe annual exempt amount, and only as much as needed to bring it down\nto that level. Example: a £4,000 gain with £10,000 of losses brought\nforward uses only £1,000 of them (£4,000 less the £3,000 exempt\namount), leaving £9,000 to carry forward.\n\n### Reliefs\n\nTwo things that widen your tax bands, which can matter if you're a\nhigher-rate taxpayer.\n\n**Personal pension contributions (relief at source).** This covers\npersonal pensions, SIPPs, and any AVC (Additional Voluntary\nContribution) paid to a separate relief-at-source provider. A toggle\nabove the field lets you enter whichever figure you actually know:\n\n- **\"I know what I paid\"** — the amount that left your own bank\n  account, *before* your provider's 20% top-up. The app grosses this\n  up for you (paid ÷ 0.8) to work out the full contribution.\n- **\"I know the total, incl. relief\"** — the full amount that landed\n  in the pension, including the 20% your provider already added. Use\n  this if that's the figure on your pension statement; the app works\n  out what you must have paid from that instead.\n\nEither way, the app widens your basic and higher-rate bands by the\nsame full contribution amount, so more of your income is taxed at 20%\nrather than 40% (or 40% rather than 45%).\n\n**Important — payslip deductions don't go here.** Most workplace\npensions, and most AVCs paid into your *main* workplace scheme, use a\n\"net pay arrangement\": the contribution comes off your pay *before*\ntax, so it's already reflected in your taxable pay above. Entering it\nhere as well would claim the same relief twice. This field is only for\ncontributions you pay out of your own already-taxed money, to a\n*separate* relief-at-source scheme. The exact mechanism differs from\nscheme to scheme, so if you're unsure which you have, check your\npayslip: if your gross pay minus the contribution equals your taxable\npay exactly, it's already net pay arrangement and belongs in\nEmployment income only, not here.\n\n**Gift Aid donations.** Enter what you actually donated. It works the\nsame way — grossed up by ÷ 0.8, widening your bands.\n\nExample: someone earning £60,270 who pays £8,000 into a personal pension\nhas a £10,000 grossed-up contribution, widening their basic-rate band\nby £10,000 and saving £2,000 in tax — shown as \"Additional pension\nrelief\" in the results, and explained fully under \"Claiming back extra\npension relief\" below. If you're a basic-rate taxpayer you'll usually\nsee £0 there, because your relief is already given in full at source.\n\nThese reliefs also reduce your \"adjusted net income\", which feeds into\nthe £100,000 Personal Allowance reduction, the Child Benefit charge, and\nstudent loan repayments.\n\n### Claiming back extra pension relief\n\nIf you pay into a relief-at-source pension or AVC and you're a\nhigher or additional-rate taxpayer, you're entitled to *more* than\nthe 20% your provider automatically adds — but that extra part isn't\nautomatic. You have to claim it yourself.\n\nThe **\"Additional pension relief\"** line in your results shows exactly\nhow much. It isn't simply 20% or 25% of your contribution — it's\nworked out by comparing your full tax position with and without the\ncontribution, so it correctly includes any knock-on reduction to your\n**High Income Child Benefit Charge** or **student loan repayment**\ntoo, if those apply to you. For some people this makes the figure\nnoticeably bigger than a flat percentage would suggest — see the\nworked examples on the app's own welcome page for a real case where a\npension contribution drops someone's income enough to restore lost\nPersonal Allowance as well, on top of the ordinary relief.\n\n**A basic-rate taxpayer will correctly see £0 here** — not a bug. If\nyour whole contribution sits within the basic-rate band, you've\nalready had the full relief you're entitled to, automatically,\nthrough the 20% top-up.\n\n**To actually get the money, once the app shows you the figure:**\n\n1. Confirm the contribution is genuinely relief-at-source — see the\n   Reliefs section above for how to check.\n2. Use the figure the app already gave you — no need to work out the\n   grossing-up by hand.\n3. Submit it to HMRC through your Self Assessment return, or via your\n   online HMRC Personal Tax Account if you don't normally file one.\n4. If you've never claimed before, you can usually backdate unclaimed\n   relief up to 4 previous tax years — worth checking past years too,\n   not just the current one.\n\nThis is general information on how the process typically works, not\npersonalised advice — HMRC's own guidance, or an accountant, can\nconfirm the right route for your specific situation.\n\n### Child Benefit\n\nEnter the total **Child Benefit received this year** — but only if\n**you** are the higher earner in your household. The High Income Child\nBenefit Charge is worked out on whichever partner earns more, not on\nwhoever claims the benefit, and not on your combined household income.\n\nIt tapers in gradually: nothing is charged up to £60,000 of adjusted net\nincome, then **1% of your Child Benefit is clawed back for every £200\nabove that**, reaching 100% at £80,000.\n\n---\n\n## Losses and unused relief, carried forward\n\nHMRC lets four things carry forward from one tax year to the next. The\napp supports all four, and offers to fill them in for you from last\nyear's saved calculation:\n\n| If this happened last year… | …enter it in this field |\n|---|---|\n| Rental expenses exceeded rental income | **Rental losses brought forward from last year** |\n| Self-employment expenses exceeded income | **Self-employment losses brought forward from last year** |\n| Mortgage interest relief was capped below the full amount paid | **Unused finance costs brought forward from last year** |\n| You had capital losses left over | **Capital losses brought forward from earlier years** |\n\nEach field sits under the section it relates to. After you calculate,\nthe results include a line giving the amount to carry forward. Save the\ncalculation and the app keeps that figure.\n\n**How the offer works.** When you calculate for a tax year and you have\na saved calculation for exactly the year before (for example a saved\n2025-26 calculation while you're calculating 2026-27), a blue box at the\ntop of the form lists what it carried forward. Click **Use these\nfigures** to fill the four fields, or **No thanks** to leave them blank.\nAfter you use them, the box tells you which saved calculation they came\nfrom, and **Undo** clears them again. You can still change any figure by\nhand.\n\n- **It never fills anything by itself.** The app asks first, because\n  guessing the wrong year would double-count a loss without you noticing.\n- **If you saved the same year more than once,** a drop-down lets you\n  choose which saved calculation to use, with the newest first.\n- **Check the figures before using them.** They are only as good as the\n  calculation you saved. Compare them with your Self Assessment return.\n  Losses only carry forward if you have reported them to HMRC (HM Revenue\n  & Customs).\n- **Changing the tax year clears figures filled in this way,** so they\n  can't stay behind under the wrong year.\n- **The offer doesn't appear while you edit a saved calculation.** If you\n  use **Edit** to correct that year's figures, its own brought-forward\n  amounts come back as they were.\n\n---\n\n## Reading your results\n\nAfter you click **Calculate**:\n\n- **Take-home / net position** — your income and gains, minus every tax\n  and deduction the app has worked out (including National Insurance,\n  the Child Benefit charge, and student loan repayments).\n- **Effective tax rate** — those deductions as a percentage of your\n  total income and gains.\n- **A coloured bar** showing where your money goes: net income, income\n  tax, National Insurance, and Capital Gains Tax, plus a grey segment\n  for your student loan repayment and/or Child Benefit charge if you\n  have them. The segments always add up to the full width.\n- **A section for each type of income**, showing the bands your income\n  fell into and the tax due in each. Tax-free amounts (your Personal\n  Savings Allowance, dividend allowance, and so on) are called out so you\n  can see what was covered.\n- **Rental and self-employment lines** stating whether the allowance or\n  your itemised expenses were used, and by how much.\n- **A Rent-a-Room line** — whether your receipts were fully tax-free, or\n  which method was used (the limit, or your itemised expenses) and by\n  how much.\n- **Mortgage interest relief** — the credit rate, the amount it was\n  based on, and the reduction to your bill.\n- **Additional pension relief** — if you entered a relief-at-source\n  pension or AVC contribution, a line showing how much *further*\n  relief you can claim back from HMRC, beyond the 20% your provider\n  already added. See \"Claiming back extra pension relief\" below for\n  what this means and how to actually get the money.\n- **National Insurance** — Class 1 and Class 4 shown separately if you\n  have both salary and self-employment income.\n- **A Class 2 note** — whether it's treated as paid for free, or whether\n  you could pay a small voluntary amount.\n- **Foreign tax credit** — how much of the tax withheld abroad was\n  credited against your UK dividend tax.\n- **Private Residence Relief** — a highlighted line showing the tax-free\n  amount on your home sale, or an amber note if it was taxed as an\n  ordinary gain because the box wasn't ticked.\n- **Capital gains detail** — this year's losses deducted first, the\n  annual exempt amount applied, and any brought-forward loss used.\n- **High Income Child Benefit Charge** — the percentage clawed back and\n  the resulting charge.\n- **Student loan repayment** — the total for the year.\n- **Marriage Allowance** — the amount added to (or taken from) your\n  allowance, or the amber eligibility warning.\n- **Check your tax code** — a collapsible panel at the bottom of the\n  results with an estimated PAYE tax code. See \"Checking your tax code\"\n  below.\n- **Carry-forward lines** — losses relieved this year, and anything\n  unused to carry into next year.\n- **A footer** showing the Personal Allowance applied (it's reduced for\n  income above £100,000) and, if you entered pension contributions or\n  Gift Aid, how much your bands were widened by.\n\n---\n\n## Checking your tax code\n\nNear the bottom of your results, **Check your tax code** shows the PAYE\ntax code your figures would normally produce, and lets you compare it\nwith the code on your payslip or HMRC letter. It appears when you have\nsalary or pension income.\n\n**What a code is.** It records your allowance, not your tax band. The\nnumber is your total tax-free allowance divided by ten, and the letter\nis your status. Most people have **1257L** (£12,570). A higher earner\nhas the same code until income passes £100,000, when the allowance\nstarts to reduce. Codes you may see:\n\n- **S** prefix: Scottish rates. **C** prefix: Welsh rates.\n- **L**: standard. **M**: you're receiving Marriage Allowance (1383M).\n  **N**: you've given Marriage Allowance to a partner (1131N).\n- **Blind Person's Allowance** is added to your allowance, so your code\n  number is higher (for example 1257L plus £3,250 is 1582L). Married\n  Couple's Allowance is not included in the estimate.\n- **K**: HMRC is adding income instead of giving an allowance, because\n  deductions are larger than your allowance.\n- **0T**: no allowance, so tax is charged from the first pound.\n\n**Things HMRC may adjust that the calculator can't see.** Add any that\napply and the estimate changes:\n\n- **State Pension.** It's paid without tax taken off, so HMRC collects\n  the tax through the code on a workplace or private pension (or job).\n  If your pension income includes State Pension, enter that part in the\n  box shown. For example, £11,973 of State Pension leaves about £597 of\n  allowance, a code of 59L.\n- **Benefits in kind**, such as a company car or private medical cover.\n- **Untaxed income** HMRC is collecting through your code.\n- **Underpaid tax** from an earlier year, converted back to allowance at\n  your tax rate.\n\n**Comparing codes.** Type the code from your payslip or letter. The\npanel says whether it matches, is within one step (rounding, £10 of\nallowance), or is higher or lower, with the approximate yearly effect\nand common reasons. It also flags a prefix or an M or N letter that\ndoesn't match what you entered.\n\n**What it doesn't do.** It's an estimate for the common cases, not\nHMRC's own calculation, and a difference is a reason to check, not\nproof of an error. Second-job codes (BR, D0, D1), emergency codes and\nmid-year changes aren't modelled. To query a code, use your personal\ntax account with HMRC.\n\n---\n\n## Managing your saved calculations\n\nSaved calculations aren't a one-way \"save and\nforget\" — you can view, correct, or remove them at any time.\n\n### Saving (create)\nAfter any result, a **Save this calculation** link appears below it.\n\n### Your saved calculations list (read)\nOnce you have at least one, a **\"Your saved calculations\"** list appears\nfurther down the page — showing the tax year, region, net income, and\nthe date each was saved.\n\n### Correcting a mistake (update)\nClick **Edit** on a saved entry to load its figures back into the form.\nA banner confirms you're editing that specific entry, and the Save\nbutton becomes **\"Update saved calculation\"** — clicking it overwrites\nthat entry rather than creating a second one. **\"Start new instead\"** in\nthe banner backs out without saving.\n\nEverything you entered comes back, including your student loan plan,\nMarriage Allowance, Child Benefit, pension contributions, Gift Aid, and\nhome-sale details. The four \"brought forward\" fields come back only when\nyou use Edit, for the reasons above.\n\n### Removing an old one (delete)\nClick **Delete** on a saved entry to remove it permanently, after a\nconfirmation prompt. This can't be undone.\n\n### How this differs from the auto-prefill\nWhen you first open the app, the form fills itself from your most recent\nsaved calculation as a convenient starting point — a banner says so, with\na **Clear** link. That's separate from editing: prefilling is a starting\npoint for a *new* calculation and never overwrites anything until you\nsave, whereas Edit updates that one saved record by design.\n\n---\n\n## What this calculator doesn't cover\n\nAt the bottom of the main page there's a panel titled **\"What this\ncalculator doesn't cover\"**. Pick a topic from its dropdown to see why\nit's excluded and where to get proper help. The topics are:\n\n**Capital gains and business**\n\n- Business Asset Disposal Relief and Investors' Relief\n- Partial Private Residence Relief (a let period, business use, or time away)\n- Foreign tax credit relief on capital gains\n- Capital allowances beyond a single vehicle (several vehicles, leasing, hire purchase)\n\n**Income, allowances and pensions**\n\n- The combined employment and self-employment National Insurance cap\n- Pension Annual Allowance tapering\n- Benefits in kind (P11D)\n- Allowances shared between partners: checking your partner's side\n\n**Residence, filing and other taxes**\n\n- Non-UK residence, the Statutory Residence Test, and the foreign income and gains regime\n- Self Assessment payments on account\n- VAT, Inheritance Tax, and property purchase taxes (Stamp Duty Land Tax, Land and Buildings Transaction Tax, Land Transaction Tax)\n\nFor most of these, the advice is to speak to a **Chartered Accountant**\n(the ICAEW has a public directory) or a **Chartered Tax Adviser**\n(Chartered Institute of Taxation). If you're not certain you're a\nstraightforward UK tax resident, resolve that first — it decides which\ntaxes apply to you at all.\n\n---\n\n## Managing tax years (administrators only)\n\nIf your account is listed as an administrator, a **Manage tax years**\nlink appears at the bottom of the page. It's where each year's HMRC\nrates are entered after the Budget, with a \"copy figures from\" option\nthat prefills every field from an earlier year so only what changed needs\nediting. That includes the student loan thresholds, the Child Benefit\ncharge thresholds, the Marriage Allowance amount, and the Rent-a-Room\nthreshold. After changing\nrates, run the project's `npm run verify -- --db` check (described in\nthe README) to confirm the live figures still produce the right answers.\n\n## Managing who can use the app (administrators only)\n\nAlongside **Manage tax years**, administrators also see a **Manage\naccess** link. This is where you control who else can sign in and use\nthe calculator at all — not who can manage rates, just who can use it.\n\n- **Administrators** are shown for reference but can't be changed from\n  this page — that list comes from your hosting configuration, and\n  changing it needs a developer.\n- **Allowed users** is the list you manage day to day. Type an email\n  address and click **Add**; click **Remove** next to an entry to take\n  someone off the list. Changes apply immediately — no waiting for a\n  deployment.\n- A brand-new setup starts with this list empty, meaning only\n  administrators can use the app until you add people here.\n- Removing someone stops them the next time they load the page or run\n  a calculation — normally within seconds — but doesn't forcibly end a\n  session already open in their browser at that exact moment.\n- If this deployment accepts payment (see \"Buying access\"), anyone who\n  paid appears here too, with a green **\"Paid £X\"** badge and the date\n  they paid — the same list, not a separate one. Removing a paid\n  entry gives you an extra warning, since it doesn't refund them\n  automatically; do that separately in the Stripe dashboard if that's\n  what you mean to do.\n\nBelow the access list, the same page also has a **\"Terms & Privacy\ndetails\"** form — the business name, address, contact email, and ICO\nregistration reference shown on the app's Terms of Service and Privacy\nPolicy pages. Fill these in here rather than editing any code; changes\nappear on those pages immediately. Until they're filled in, visitors\nsee a plain placeholder; administrators see a reminder of exactly\nwhat's still missing.\n\n---\n\n## Good to know / limitations\n\n- **This is an estimate**, not a substitute for professional advice or\n  an official HMRC calculation.\n- Results are shown in whole pounds.\n- **Enter salary as taxable pay** (your P60 figure). Don't also enter\n  payslip pension deductions under Reliefs — that would claim the relief\n  twice.\n- Rental and self-employment deductions use the allowance or a\n  total you itemise. The app doesn't check what counts as an allowable\n  expense, so use HMRC's guidance if unsure whether a cost qualifies.\n- Rent-a-Room Relief covers a furnished room in your own home only.\n  Asking HMRC to use the limit method, and claiming a loss from lodger\n  income, are not handled — the app shows the result either way, but it\n  can't tell HMRC for you.\n- Losses and unused relief are offered from last year's saved\n  calculation, but only when you click to use them. The app can't tell\n  whether the saved figures match what HMRC holds for you.\n- If you have both salary and self-employment income, HMRC caps your\n  combined National Insurance. The app works out Class 1 and Class 4\n  separately, so your real bill may be slightly lower.\n- Foreign tax credit covers dividends only, not capital gains, and the\n  app credits whatever you enter — make sure it reflects the correct\n  treaty rate rather than a higher, un-relieved withholding.\n- Capital Gains Tax uses the standard 18%/24% rates. Business Asset\n  Disposal Relief isn't modelled, and Private Residence Relief covers\n  only the full-relief case.\n- The Child Benefit charge assumes you're the higher earner in your\n  household if you enter any Child Benefit at all.\n- Marriage Allowance can be modelled for either side, one person at a\n  time. The app can't check your partner's side of the same transfer or\n  combine the two figures for the couple.\n- Student loan repayments are estimated on your total income; your\n  payroll deductions may differ slightly during the year.\n- Only the tax years your administrator has loaded are available.\n- Saving or editing restores the *totals* for itemised expenses, not the\n  individual categories — re-enter that breakdown if you want it.\n- Deleting a saved calculation is permanent.\n\n---\n\n## Quick FAQ\n\n**Do I need to sign in?**\nYes — the whole app requires sign-in with an approved Google account,\nnot just saving. If your account isn't on the approved list, you'll\nsee a message rather than the calculator, along with either a \"Buy\naccess\" button (if this deployment accepts payment) or instructions to\nask your administrator to add your email address — sometimes both.\n\n**I paid for access but the calculator still isn't showing.**\nGive it a few seconds — the app needs a moment to record your payment\nafter Stripe confirms it, and a page refresh usually resolves it. If\nit's still not unlocked after a minute or two, contact whoever\nadministers this app; there may be an issue with how payments are\nconfigured on their end, not with your payment itself (Stripe will\nhave charged you either way — check your email for their receipt).\n\n**What does \"Additional pension relief\" actually mean?**\nIt's the extra tax relief you're owed on a relief-at-source pension or\nAVC contribution, beyond the 20% your provider already added\nautomatically — only basic-rate relief happens by itself; higher and\nadditional-rate relief has to be claimed. See \"Claiming back extra\npension relief\" above for the full explanation and how to actually\nclaim it from HMRC.\n\n**Should I enter what I paid, or the total contribution including\nrelief?**\nEither — there's a toggle above the pension field for exactly this.\nPick whichever figure you actually have in front of you; the app\nconverts between them for you either way.\n\n**I let a room in my home to a lodger — which section do I use?**\nRent-a-Room, not Rental. Rental is for a separate property you let as a\nlandlord; Rent-a-Room is only for a furnished room in the home you live\nin. Up to £7,500 a year (£3,750 each if you share the income) is\ntax-free automatically.\n\n**Do I need to tell HMRC about lodger income?**\nNot if your receipts are within the limit — the relief applies\nautomatically, and you don't need to report the income or file a return\nfor it alone. Over the limit, you must report it through Self\nAssessment.\n\n**I share the lodger's rent with my partner — what do I enter?**\nTick the \"I share this income\" box and enter only your own share of the\nreceipts. The limit then drops to £3,750 each, applied to your share.\n\n**My costs for having a lodger are more than the limit — can I claim\nthem?**\nYes. Once your receipts are over the limit, HMRC taxes your actual\nprofit (receipts minus expenses) unless you ask for the limit method, so\nclaiming expenses needs no special step. The app shows when expenses\nbeat the limit. It doesn't model a loss if your expenses exceed your\nreceipts.\n\n**Why do my dividends use UK-wide rates when I'm Scottish?**\nIncome tax on salary, pension, rental, and self-employment income is\ndevolved to Scotland. Savings, dividends, and Capital Gains Tax aren't,\nso they use the same rates everywhere.\n\n**I pay into a workplace pension — do I enter it under Reliefs?**\nUsually not. If the contribution comes off your payslip before tax (a\n\"net pay arrangement\", the most common set-up), it's already reflected\nin your P60 pay figure. Only contributions you pay from your own taxed\nincome into a personal pension or SIPP go under Reliefs. If you're not\nsure which yours is, ask the pension provider.\n\n**Why can't I put my pension income under Employment income?**\nThe income tax would be the same, but Employment income also triggers\nNational Insurance, which pensions never attract.\n\n**I have a mortgage — why does entering it lower my tax less than I\nexpected?**\nSince 2020/21 the relief is a flat 20% credit on your final bill rather\nthan a deduction at your marginal rate. Higher-rate taxpayers lose most:\nthey used to get 40% or 45% relief and now get 20%.\n\n**Should I itemise my expenses or use the allowance?**\nYou don't need to decide. Enter your real expenses and the app uses\nwhichever is worth more, for both rental and self-employment.\n\n**Why is National Insurance lower on self-employment profit than on the\nsame amount of salary?**\nClass 4 is 6% on profit between £12,570 and £50,270 (2% above), against\n8% for Class 1 on salary.\n\n**I have US shares — how does the foreign tax credit work?**\nWith a W-8BEN on file, the US withholds 15%. Enter that in \"Foreign tax\nalready withheld on these dividends\" and it's credited against your UK\ndividend tax, up to what the UK would charge on that dividend — never\nmore, and never as a refund.\n\n**I sold my home. What do I do?**\nEnter the gain under \"Gain on selling your only or main home\". If it was\nyour main home for the whole time you owned it, tick the box and the gain\nis exempt. If not, leave it unticked — you'll get a cautious estimate,\nand a sale with a let period or business use is worth taking advice on.\n\n**Why did I get an amber Marriage Allowance warning?**\nIf you ticked that you're receiving, your own income looks too high to\nqualify. HMRC's limit is £50,270 in the rest of the UK (£43,662 in\nScotland), measured before the transfer. If you ticked that you're\ngiving, your income is not below your Personal Allowance, so you may not\nqualify to give any away. The figures are still shown, but they may not\nreflect what HMRC would allow.\n\n**I gave my Marriage Allowance away and now I'm paying tax. Is that\nwrong?**\nNo. After giving £1,260 you keep £11,310 of allowance, so income above\nthat is taxed. The results show how much income is affected. Your\npartner's saving, up to £252, appears on their own calculation, so the\ncouple can still come out ahead overall.\n\n**Why is there a blue box offering figures from last year?**\nYou have a saved calculation for the previous tax year that carried\nforward a loss or unused relief. Click **Use these figures** to fill\nthem in, or **No thanks**. See \"Losses and unused relief, carried\nforward\" above.\n\n**Why is my student loan repayment different from my payslip?**\nThe app estimates the whole year on your total income. Payroll works\nperiod by period, and only on your pay from that employer, so the two\ncan differ. Any difference is settled through Self Assessment or your\ntax code.\n\n**Why don't my results match a worked example to the penny?**\nResults are displayed to the nearest pound. The underlying calculation\nis exact to the penny — the validation guide quotes pence, so compare\nafter rounding.\n\n**How do I start again from scratch?**\nClick **Clear all**, next to the Calculate button. It empties the whole\nform and the results, but leaves your tax year and region alone, and it\nnever deletes anything you've saved.\n\n**I made a mistake in a calculation I already saved — do I need to save\na duplicate?**\nNo. Click **Edit** on it in \"Your saved calculations\", correct the\nfigures, and click **Update saved calculation**.\n\n**What's the difference between the auto-prefill and clicking Edit?**\nAuto-prefill is a starting point for a *new* calculation and saves\nnothing until you choose. Edit puts you in a mode where Save updates that\nexact record.\n\n**I deleted a saved calculation by mistake — can I get it back?**\nNo. Deletion is permanent.\n\n**The tax year I want isn't in the dropdown.**\nAsk your app administrator to add it on the **Manage tax years** page —\nnew years are added after each Budget.\n\n**My situation isn't covered — what should I do?**\nCheck the \"What this calculator doesn't cover\" panel at the bottom of the\npage. For anything in it, or anything unusual, speak to a Chartered\nAccountant or Chartered Tax Adviser.\n";
